{:edb/db-before-t 2159 :edb/db-after-t 2160 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535472 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:45:32.384Z" 13194139535472 true] [17592186192311 1100 "Consider the curve $x^3-\\ln y=1$. Given that $x=x(t)$, $y=y(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $x=1$, find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point." 13194139535472 false] [17592186192311 1100 "Consider the curve $x^3-\\ln y=1.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $x=1,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point." 13194139535472 true] [17592186192311 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - \\ln y &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(x^{3} - \\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(x^{3}) - \\frac{d}{dt}(\\ln y) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dy}(\\ln y) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot 3x^{2} - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{1}{y} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2\n$$\n\nand $x=1$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{1}{y} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y$, but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - \\ln y &= 1 \\\\\n1 - \\ln y &= 1 \\\\\n\\ln y &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 false] [17592186192311 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2$ and $x=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1 \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 true] [17592186192312 1100 "Consider the following ellipse:\n\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}=1$\n\nGiven that $x=x(t)$, $y=y(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ on the curve at the point $(1,\\sqrt2)$, find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point." 13194139535472 false] [17592186192312 1100 "Consider the following ellipse: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}=1\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ on the curve at the point $(1,\\sqrt2),$ find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point." 13194139535472 true] [17592186192312 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4}) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}) + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{y^{2}}{4}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}) + \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dy}(\\frac{y^{2}}{4}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (x) + \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot (\\frac{y}{2}) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\n$x=1$, and\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt2\n$$\n\nand solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (1) + \\sqrt{2} \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 false] [17592186192312 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{y}{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2},$ $x=1,$ and $y=\\sqrt2,$ and solve $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.$  We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1)+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 true] [17592186192325 1100 "Consider the section of the hyperbola\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{3}-\\dfrac{y^2}{2}=1$\nthat lies in the first quadrant. Given that $x=x(t)$, $y=y(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=3$, find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at this point." 13194139535472 false] [17592186192325 1100 "Consider the section of the hyperbola \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{3}-\\dfrac{y^2}{2}=1\n$$\n\nthat lies in the first quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=3,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at this point." 13194139535472 true] [17592186192325 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{3} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x^{2}}{3} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x^{2}}{3}) - \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{y^{2}}{2}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{2}}{3}) - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dy}(\\frac{y^{2}}{2}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (\\frac{2x}{3}) - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2\n$$\n\nand $x=3$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (\\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{3}) - (-2) \\cdot y &= 0 \\\\\n2\\frac{dx}{dt} + 2y &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y$, but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=3$ into the equation of the hyperbola.\n\nAt the point where $x=3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{3} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{3^{2}}{3} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= 2 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4 \\\\\ny &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y > 0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant.\n\nFinally, substituting $y= 2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -y \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 false] [17592186192325 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2$ and $x=3.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2\\cdot 3}{3})-(-2)\\cdot y & =0 \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also want to substitute for $y$, but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=3$ into the equation of the hyperbola.   \n\nAt the point where $x=3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y > 0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $y= 2,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 true] [17592186192587 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350830 13194139535472 true] [17592186192587 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350851 13194139535472 true] [17592186192587 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350858 13194139535472 true] [17592186192587 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350889 13194139535472 true] [17592186192588 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350837 13194139535472 true] [17592186192588 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350844 13194139535472 true] [17592186192588 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350892 13194139535472 true] [17592186192588 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350895 13194139535472 true] [17592186192588 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350898 13194139535472 true] [17592186192599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350865 13194139535472 true] [17592186192599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350872 13194139535472 true] [17592186192599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350879 13194139535472 true] [17592186192599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350886 13194139535472 true] [17592186192599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350901 13194139535472 true] [17592186193162 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535472 true] [17592186193162 1100 "Consider the curve $x + \\ln y = 4$. Given that $x = x(t)$, $y = y(t)$, and that $\\frac{dx}{dt} =-1$ on the curve at the point where $x = 4$, find the value of $\\frac{dy}{dt}$ at that point." 13194139535472 false] [17592186193162 1100 "Consider the curve $x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=4.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-1$ on the curve at the point where $x=4,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." 13194139535472 true] [17592186193162 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-1$ and $x=4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=4$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4 \\\\ 4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350830 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350831 13194139535472 true] [17592186350830 1089 "q-115951" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350830 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535472 true] [17592186350830 1100 "Consider the curve ${e}^{x}-{e}^{y}=y.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3$ on the curve at the point where $y=0,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." 13194139535472 true] [17592186350830 1113 #uuid "e69b3edd-f7b5-5b53-8fa2-bbbd0997ab35" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350830 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & =y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x}-{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{x}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{x} & =\\fra