({y}^{2}+2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y+2) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2y+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y & =-x \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y & =-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2y+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(2\\cdot (-1)+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(0) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350891 13194139535472 true] [17592186350890 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535472 true] [17592186350890 1041 "field-1" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350890 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350891 13194139535472 true] [17592186350890 1110 #uuid "0e932c59-180a-5f69-865e-cf93e19d919e" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350891 1034 "0" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350891 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535472 true] [17592186350891 1111 #uuid "489a1c30-b881-5af4-b75c-cebd5f0538ac" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350892 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350893 13194139535472 true] [17592186350892 1089 "q-343557" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350892 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535472 true] [17592186350892 1100 "Consider the parabola ${y}^{2}-20x=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{10}$ on the curve at the point $(5,10),$ find the value of  $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350892 1113 #uuid "ccdec465-41c1-53e9-9934-e5b569cf900d" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350892 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-20x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}-20x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{10},$ $x=5,$ and $y=10,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot 10)-(\\frac{3}{10})\\cdot 20 & =0 \\\\ 20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-6 & =0 \\\\ 20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350893 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350894 13194139535472 true] [17592186350893 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535472 true] [17592186350893 1041 "field-1" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350893 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350894 13194139535472 true] [17592186350893 1110 #uuid "81eb7b30-0e4b-5a53-9b7d-95ede5b95068" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350894 1034 "\\frac{3}{10}" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350894 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535472 true] [17592186350894 1111 #uuid "f5949430-27b3-53fb-8a6c-2e394adc535e" 13194139535472 true] [17592186350895 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350896 13